<section class="section story-section" aria-labelledby="story-title"><div class="shell story-layout"><div class="story-sticky"><p class="eyebrow">FROM A QUESTION TO AN EXPERIMENT</p><h2 id="story-title">The world changes.<br/><em>What changes inside?</em></h2><div class="story-diagram" aria-hidden="true"><span>WORLD</span><i></i><span class="story-active">INTERNAL MODEL</span><i></i><span>ACTION</span></div><p class="muted">An illustration of the problem, not a claim that the demonstration below trains an intelligent system.</p></div><div class="story-steps"><article class="story-step reveal"><span class="mono">01 / PERCEIVE</span><h3>Only part of the world is visible.</h3><p>An observation is local. An internal representation can contain assumptions that have not been checked recently.</p></article><article class="story-step reveal"><span class="mono">02 / PREDICT</span><h3>A plan depends on a model.</h3><p>A route makes sense only under the agent’s current map. A clear plan can still rest on a mistaken assumption.</p></article><article class="story-step reveal"><span class="mono">03 / CONFRONT</span><h3>Reality disagrees.</h3><p>A passage closes. The observer sees the change immediately, but the agent must encounter evidence before revising its representation.</p></article><article class="story-step reveal"><span class="mono">04 / REVISE</span><h3>Change the representation, then the plan.</h3><p>The laboratory makes that separation explicit: observe, detect a contradiction, update the model, replan, act.</p></article></div></div></section>
